## Purpose

A project somebody founded has to be two things at once: **working**, so the need is actually served, and **joinable**, so someone new can get in and help. Both are easy to claim and hard to fake, and the only honest tests are the direct ones. Working is tested by walking the software, live, in front of people. Joinable is tested by handing the repository to a stranger with no one to ask.

That's true of any project you'll ever work on. A sprint review, a release demo, and a pitch to a sponsor all ask the first question. Every new teammate and every would-be contributor asks the second, usually without telling you. *(In this course, both tests are <course-link type="assignment" id="cp10">CP10</course-link>, and both happen today.)*

You'll leave with the most useful feedback you'll get before launch: a stranger's record of exactly where your front door stuck, and your classmates' read on what your demo proved.

---

## Learning objectives

By the end of this session, you'll be able to:

1. **Demo working software**: claim first, honest about the edges, inside the time box.
2. **Tell proving a claim from showing one**, in your own demo and in someone else's.
3. **Audit an unfamiliar repository** methodically, and record what happened faithfully.
4. **Turn demo and audit feedback into a ranked fix list** for the launch.

---

## What we'll do together

**Setup (3 minutes).** Find your audit partner and their repository URL on the board, and start cloning their tag right away, so a slow network doesn't eat the audit later:

```
git clone --branch cp10 https://github.com/THEIR-ORG/THEIR-REPO.git
```

**Progress demos (whole room).** Each project gets **six minutes to demo, two minutes of questions, and one minute to change presenters.** The time box is hard: a demo that runs long takes someone else's time.

1. **Open with the claim.** One sentence, first: "Version 1 proves a volunteer can find an item and check it out."
2. **Walk the slice:** end to end, live, from the tag, on real data. The path in your slice statement.
3. **Name one honest edge:** something that doesn't work yet, said before anyone has to ask.
4. **If it breaks,** run your fallback calmly. No restarts, and no apologizing for the rest of the demo.

**Proven or shown?** Every demo either proves its claim or only shows it, and one question tells them apart:

> **If the claim were false, would the demo have looked different?**
> Yes: it was **proven**. No: it was only **shown**.

A demo **proves** a claim when it could have failed in front of you. The real code runs on real data, live, end to end. In Prop Loft's case: search finds the item, the checkout saves, the list now says who has it, and a second checkout of the same item is refused. Each of those would look different if the code didn't work.

A demo only **shows** a claim when you'd see the same thing either way: a screen with hardcoded data, screenshots of what it will do, "and then this would save to the database," or ten screens toured with nothing connected behind them.

A claim you say plainly isn't proven yet ("we haven't put it in front of a wardrobe lead") is neither. It's **honest**, and it counts in your favor. A tour that implies more than the code does counts against you. It's the same test as the falsifier in your MVP Plan, turned around: if no result could have shown you were wrong, nothing was proven.

**While you watch,** fill in a **Demo Feedback Sheet** for each demo: the claim it set out to prove, whether it was proven or only shown and what convinced you, how the walk went, and the question you'd still ask. The sheets are anonymous, and they're feedback for the presenter, not a grade. Hand yours over when that presenter's questions end. Ask questions in the same spirit: *did that prove it, or show it?* Asked kindly, answered directly.

**The repo audit (30 minutes, everyone at once).** Every project audits its partner's repository while the partner audits theirs, working through the **Repo Audit Sheet** out loud:

1. **Get it running** from their README, exactly as written. Each step: as written, a guess or workaround, wrong or missing, or couldn't.
2. **Find your way:** say what the project is, pick a good first issue, and find the code you'd change.
3. **Read the history:** the commit log, pull requests, board, known issues, and where to ask.
4. **Log every stall:** the step, the exact error or confusing line, and what you did.

Work from the docs alone. There's no one to ask, and that's the point: a future contributor won't have anyone either. The sheet records facts, not a score. Sign it and hand it to me; I'll photograph it and give it to the authors.

**Debrief (4 minutes).** With your audit sheet and your demo feedback in hand, write a **top-five fix list**, ranked by launch impact: what would stop a newcomer from joining, or a user from getting through? Then, round the room: *what was the most surprising thing a stranger tripped on?*

